    "logic": {
      "concept": "Intraday order flow overshoots. A burst of one-sided activity pushes price a measurable distance from EMA(20), and the liquidity consumed to do it is replaced nearer the average. The ATR multiple is what makes 'far' comparable across contracts and volatility regimes; the Stochastic cross is evidence the reversion has BEGUN rather than a prediction that it will. Trades are attributed by their ENTRY bar, never the exit, so a scalp opened in one quadrant that closes in another still scores where it opened.",
      "entry": "LONG when Low dipped below EMA(20) - 1.5 x ATR(14) within the last 5 bars, Close crosses ABOVE the EMA, and Stochastic %K crosses above %D having been <= 20.0. The two crosses need only arrive within 3 bars of each other; the entry fires on the later. SHORT mirrors it: High above EMA + 1.5 x ATR, Close crossing BELOW the EMA, %K crossing below %D from >= 80.0.",
      "exit": "The ATR bracket, whichever side the bar reaches first: a target at 1.0 x ATR(14) and a stop at 1.0 x ATR(14) from the fill (trailing=False). With use_stoch_exit the position also closes on the opposite Stochastic cross."
    }
  },
